 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +18.1 & [+8.4, +29.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \checkmark \\[2pt]
QwQ & L63 & 384d & 5 & +13.0 & +19.2 & [+9.3, +33.5] & $-$15.0 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +9.4 & [$-$7.1, +27.7] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\[2pt]
R1-32B & L63 & 384d & 1 & +18.7 & +15.4 & [+8.7, +22.6] & $-$14.6 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +11.7 & [$-$0.2, +24.3] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{HS vs.\ text at threshold-based layers (LR probes, 60/20/20 split, 10{,}000 bootstrap resamples). Enc.: text encoder (384d = MiniLM-L6-v2, 1024d = BGE-large). $\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp); ``--'' = BA and FPR are not computed for the 1024d comparison because the 384d and 1024d encoders operate at different probe calibration points and do not share a decision threshold. HB: \checkmark{} = model-level test passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$); all 384d model-level tests pass$^\dagger$. 1024d: HB over 4 model-level precision tests.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

The HS advantage is encoder-conditional: against BGE-large (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB correction (\cref{app:encoder_robustness}). Multi-seed stability appears in \cref{app:multi_seed}. Against the 384d baseline, HS probes outperform text probes in all four models$^\dagger$ (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}): confirmed at 7--8B (OT-7B: +21.9pp precision, R1-8B: +20.1pp), significant but position-competitive at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ (+19.2pp), and exploratory at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (+15.4pp, position-mediated). R1-8B FPR is directionally consistent (5/5 seeds) but not robust: $p{=}0.044$ (rank 12/12 in the HB step-down, the weakest surviving test), with only 1/5 seeds passing Bonferroni correction (\cref{app:multi_seed}). R1-32B passes 5/5 seeds ($\Delta$Prec std${=}2.0$pp) but its advantage is position-mediated (residualized gap NS; \cref{app:position_residual}); QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). Overall, 18/20 model-seed combinations pass full HB (\cref{app:multi_seed}). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 11.1--16.8pp above chance (\cref{app:control}); selectivity measures label structure (main vs.\ shuffled), complementary to position contribution (temporal artifacts). Position features account for 9--20pp of balanced accuracy (\cref{app:position_residual}); R1-32B's HS advantage is fully explained by position (residualized gap +1.9pp, $p{=}0.914$).

\paragraph{Capacity control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (79--92\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage across all four models$^\dagger$ (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$, all HB). Two individual metrics are non-significant: R1-8B BA (+1.3pp, $p{=}0.731$) and QwQ-32B FPR ($-$11.4pp, $p{=}0.038$, below HB threshold). The control provides partial evidence against a pure capacity explanation but does not rule out representation quality differences. Random projection to 384d yields similar results ($\Delta$Prec +8.3 to +20.5pp; \cref{app:random_projection}), further weakening the capacity explanation. E5-Mistral-7B (4096d$\rightarrow$PCA 384d) yields intermediate results (2/4 HB; \cref{app:e5_mistral}), forming a monotonic encoder-quality gradient with MiniLM (4/4) and BGE-large (1/4).

\paragraph{Encoder robustness.} Against BGE-large~\citep{xiao2023cpack} (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB (1/4 precision); R1-8B shows adverse FPR direction (+1.2pp). The advantage is encoder-conditional (\cref{fig:forest_precision}); layer selection (Contribution 1) is encoder-independent.

\paragraph{Position mediation.} Position ($t/T$) accounts for 9--20pp of balanced accuracy,\footnote{R1-32B's 20.4pp drop reflects $W{=}1$; at $W{\geq}3$, drops are 9--17pp.} but all models retain non-positional signal above chance (all $p{<}0.001$; \cref{app:position_residual}). Three of four models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (residualized gap +3.7 to +6.9pp, HB corrected); R1-32B's gap is +1.9pp ($p{=}0.914$, $n{=}20$). Temporal windowing reduces position dependence (24.3pp drop at $W{=}3$ vs.\ 16.5pp at $W{=}15$).

\paragraph{Caveats.} Nested 5-fold cross-validation confirms the HS precision advantage for both 7--8B models (10/10 folds; \cref{app:nested_cv}), with shrinkage of 13--25\% relative to holdout estimates, ruling out hyperparameter overfitting. R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ with 25\% coverage (5/20 traces); at $W{\geq}3$, dimensionality ($51200 \times W$ for $n{=}20$) prevents significance. Sweeping regularization $C$ over $[0.01, 100]$ yields ${<}2$pp variation in balanced accuracy; the HS advantage is also stable across decision thresholds (BA variation ${<}1.5$pp across $\tau \in [0.3, 0.7]$; \cref{app:threshold_sensitivity}). QwQ-32B HS lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps vs.\ text 3.7--12.3 steps): higher precision at later detection. On benign traces, probes produce 11--50\% false alarm rates (model-dependent), so current methods characterize the commitment phenomenon rather than constitute production-ready detectors. Per-model details in \cref{sec:32b-analysis}.

\subsection{32B Model-Specific Analysis}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

Both 32B results are exploratory ($n{=}19$--$20$ precludes nested CV, as each fold would contain only 4 traces per class). We report effect sizes as descriptive rather than confirmatory; LOO-CV and multi-seed analyses (\cref{app:layer_sweep,app:multi_seed}) provide partial robustness checks but do not substitute for independent validation. By contrast, nested CV on the 7--8B models shows 13--25\% shrinkage (\cref{app:nested_cv}), confirming those estimates. R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the Qwen-32B architecture (64 layers, 5120 hidden) but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}); training method is the likely differentiator.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Cov.} & \textbf{Lead (steps)} & \textbf{HB} & \textbf{Pos.\ margin} \\
\midrule
R1-32B & 25\% & +11.7 to +13.3 & \checkmark{} ($W{=}1$) & +14.2pp \\
QwQ-32B & 74\% & $-$1.2 to +1.9 & \checkmark{} (all $W$) & $-$3.6pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{32B cross-model divergence. R1-32B provides limited early detection with narrow coverage; QwQ-32B provides robust significance with poor lead time. HB: \checkmark{} = model-level test passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$). Pos.\ margin: HS balanced accuracy minus position-only baseline.}
\label{tab:32b_divergence}
\end{table}

R1-32B detects commitment 11--13 steps early with a +14.2pp margin over the position baseline, which confirms genuine representational signal, but covers only 25\% of traces at the single significant window ($W{=}1$). QwQ-32B achieves statistical significance at all window sizes with 74\% coverage, but HS probes fire later than text probes (negative lead time). Consistent with the universal position analysis (\cref{sec:hs-text}), QwQ-32B's position baseline achieves higher balanced accuracy (0.832 vs.\ 0.796). HS precision (+4.5pp, $p{=}0.133$) and FPR (+2.5pp, $p{=}0.279$) margins over the position baseline are directionally positive but not statistically significant at $n{=}19$; minimum detectable effects at 80\% power range from 8.6 to 9.5pp for 32B models (\cref{app:power_analysis}). The divergence implies that per-model validation is essential: no single detection configuration generalizes even within the same architecture at the same scale, and a system validated on one 32B model would fail on the other.

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities (\cref{fig:window_ablation}): text precision +8 to +23pp, HS +1.7 to +12.9pp from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$. At 7--8B, the modality advantage (+20.1 to +21.9pp) exceeds temporal improvement (+13pp); both effects are complementary. GRU models achieve comparable accuracy but higher FPR (\cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds}); full per-model ablations in \cref{app:temporal_details}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_forest_precision.pdf}
\caption{Forest plot of HS precision advantage over text probes. Blue: vs.\ MiniLM-384d; orange: vs.\ BGE-1024d. Solid markers: survives Holm-Bonferroni; hollow: does not. All 384d comparisons are significant (strong to qualified evidence); against BGE-1024d, only OT-7B (strong) survives correction.}
\label{fig:forest_precision}
\end{figure}

CP-aligned probe score trajectories (\cref{fig:trajectory} in Appendix) confirm that HS probes exhibit a sharper score transition at the commitment point than text probes, with OT-7B showing the clearest separation and R1-32B the weakest (consistent with the evidence gradient).

\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

No alternative method consistently matches supervised probing (\cref{tab:negative}). BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian}/CUSUM fail (0\% detection on 3/4 models; \cref{app:bocpd_config}), inherently limited on short reasoning chains where gradual transitions never trigger hazard functions; Mahalanobis distance yields near-zero separation, consistent with directional rather than distributional safety signal; RepE~\citep{zou2023representation} is model-dependent (QwQ-32B BA 0.756 vs.\ R1-32B 0.559); MLP probes overfit; CCS misaligns with temporal structure; cross-model transfer fails. These failures define four requirements for CP detection: the signal is directional (Mahalanobis fails), gradual (BOCPD/CUSUM fail), model-specific (transfer fails), and linearly separable (MLP overfits). Supervised linear probes with per-model training and principled layer selection form the minimal effective methodology. Details in \cref{app:negative_details}.

